draw the game when the direct path cannot

A frame whose display list is nothing but `Draw::Game` takes the direct path:
`draw_list` returns without touching the canvas, because the game is going
straight to the panel from `output_frame` instead. That is the fast path this
device leans on -- it saves a whole canvas copy per frame.

`output_frame` then decides whether it can really do that, and it has four
conditions, not one: the canvas has to match the rect, the panel has to want
the byte order the canvas holds, and the blue-light gain has to be identity.
When any of them said no, the direct write was skipped and the panel was
painted from a canvas that nothing had drawn into. The frame came out as the
surround and no game.

Blue light is what found it, because turning the filter on is the only one of
those conditions a user can switch: reported from a Miyoo Mini Plus as "black,
or just the shaders". The others are latent on hardware this port has not met
-- a panel wanting RGB rather than BGR would do the same thing with the filter
off.

So the decision is made once and, when the direct write cannot happen, the
game is drawn into the canvas before the copy. The fast path is unchanged when
it applies.

The test drives `output_frame` against memory the caller owns, through a
`#[cfg(test)]` hook on the framebuffer's current target, and fails against the
old code with "the panel came out blank: 0 lit pixels of 421120" -- which is
the bug as the handheld showed it.

A second, cheaper fix is left for later: the gain could be folded into the
direct write as a lookup per channel, so blue light keeps the fast path rather
than costing a full extra pass over the frame.

Applies to upstream 91499a9.

